-- title:
--   Hecke-equivariant bounded-kernel map on Eisenstein torsion killed by reduction
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, let $B$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbf Q}=\mathrm{AlgebraicClosure}\,\mathbf Q$ lying over $2$ in the sense that $2$ is a nonunit of $B$, and assume the predicate `ReductionInputsModL B p`, i.e. `ReductionInputsAlong` for $B$ together with its residue map at level $p$. The group $\mathrm{JZero}\,p$, the degree-zero divisor class group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the modular function field of level $p$ over $\overline{\mathbf Q}$, carries the Hecke action `heckeModuleBar p` of $\mathbb T=\mathrm{MvPolynomial}\ \mathrm{Nat.Primes}\ \mathbf Z$. The assertion is that there is a natural number $C$, independent of $m$, such that for every $m$ there is an additive homomorphism $g$ from the subgroup $\mathrm{eisensteinTorsionBar}\,p\,2\,m \sqcap \ker(\mathrm{reductionModL}\,B\,p)$ — the elements of $\mathrm{JZero}\,p$ annihilated by every element of $(\mathrm{eisensteinMaximalIdeal}\,p\,2)^m$ and killed by reduction along $B$ — to the quotient of $\mathrm{heckeLatticeAlgebra}\,p\,\emptyset$ (the image of the action homomorphism of the weight-two Hecke algebra on the integral lattice, a $\mathbf Z$-subalgebra of its endomorphisms) by the ideal generated by $2^m$, with two properties: equivariance, stated pointwise, namely for all $t\in\mathbb T$, all $z$ in the subgroup and any proof that $t\cdot z$ again lies in the subgroup, $g(t\cdot z)$ equals the class of $(\mathrm{latticeRestrictHom}\,p\,\emptyset)(\mathrm{heckeEvalForms}\,p\,2\,(t))$ times $g(z)$, where `heckeEvalForms` sends the variable at a prime $\ell$ to $U_\ell$ if $\ell\mid p$ and to $T_\ell$ otherwise; and the finiteness bound $\mathrm{Nat.card}\,\ker g\le 2^{C}$.
--
--   This is the form in which Mazur's analysis of the ordinary filtration of the $2$-divisible group attached to the Eisenstein quotient of $J_0(p)$ enters the argument: the part of the Eisenstein torsion killed by reduction at a place above $2$ is coordinatised, Hecke-equivariantly and up to a kernel of bounded order, by the Hecke lattice algebra modulo $2^m$. It is used to bound the order of that intersection in terms of the Hecke lattice quotient.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_exists_addMonoidHom_inf_ker_reductionModL_eisensteinTorsionBar_heckeLatticeAlgebra_quotient_two_pow_natCard_ker_le.lean

